 Good Tuesday afternoon. Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 591 words, a 2-min. read. Thanks to Sheryl Miller for copy editing . 

 💊 Breaking: Eric Cantor, a former House Republican leader, was named president and CEO of PhRMA, the voice of the pharmaceutical industry, which could face increased scrutiny over drug pricing if Democrats win power in the midterms. 

 Cantor, 63, succeeds Steve Ubl, who announced in April that he was stepping down after 11 years. Cantor, a Republican from the Richmond area, is vice chair and managing director at investment bank Moelis &amp; Company. He's a frequent guest on CNBC's "Squawk Box." Go deeper . 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Meet OpenAI&#x27;s new agent, &quot;dots&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Screenshot: OpenAI ChatGPT maker OpenAI today launched "dots" — AI agents with disarmingly cute avatars that can be asked to perform a range of tasks, Axios' Ina Fried reports. 

 👾 Powered by GPT-6 Astra, dots can work across connected apps, Codex and ChatGPT Work to do research, analyze data, prepare documents and build software.

 They can work in the background and reach out when they need more input. Users can also set boundaries around dots' actions. Dots are available to OpenAI's Pro, Business and Enterprise users, with one dot per user to start. OpenAI says in an announcement: "Your dot is always within reach, whether it is through ChatGPT, Slack, or Teams."

 "You can ask questions, explore ideas, and give feedback along the way; or you can simply hop on a voice call when you need to talk something out." (Go deeper.) 🤝 OpenAI says it envisions teams of dots eventually working together.

 CEO Sam Altman said at a developer event today: "This is the real deal version of AI we've always imagined." 🖼️ The big picture: OpenAI's dots will compete with a growing crop of consumer AI assistants used to book travel, cancel subscriptions and more, including Meta's Muse agent.

 Meta dove headfirst into the consumer market, launching Muse widely to its massive audience. The app topped the download charts early on. OpenAI is targeting high-end subscribers, mostly developers — at least for now. Read the announcement ... Go deeper . 

 
 
